This is a more concluded documentation for my work on this project

BLACKHOLE is a high-performance C++ relativistic ray tracing engine designed to simulate gravitational lensing, 
light deflection and accretion disk dynamics in curved spacetime around massive compact objects.

FeaturesGeneral Relativity Solvers: Ray-marching along null geodesics in Schwarzschild (non-rotating) 
and Kerr (rotating) metrics using Runge-Kutta integration (RK4 / RKF45).Accretion Disk Rendering: Procedural and textured accretion disks with relativistic Doppler beaming 
and gravitational redshift effects.Camera Systems: Pinhole and curved-space cameras supporting adaptive step-size integration for high numerical accuracy near the photon sphere.
Multi-threaded Architecture: Highly parallelized ray tracing utilizing OpenMP and C++20 standard concurrency primitives.Output Export: High-dynamic-range (HDR) rendering output exported directly to .ppm, .png, or .exr file formats.
Technical OverviewThe engine integrates the geodesic equation in curved spacetime governed by the metric tensor $g_{\mu\nu}$:$$\frac{d^2 x^\mu}{d\lambda^2} + \Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta} \frac{dx^\alpha}{d\lambda} \frac{dx^\beta}{d\lambda} = 0$$Where:$x^\mu$ represents the spacetime position coordinates $(t, r, \theta, \phi)$.$\lambda$ is the affine parameter along the photon path.$\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}$ denotes the Christoffel symbols derived from the spacetime metric.
